🧩 Delegate vs Interface in Java

| Concept             | **Delegate**                                                             | **Interface**                                                                        |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **Definition**      | Design approach where one object **hands off** a task to another object  | A **contract** (methods) that a class must implement                                 |
| **Purpose**         | **Behavior reuse** – reuse functionality by delegating to another object | **Abstraction** – define shared behaviors multiple classes must follow               |
| **Main Use**        | Code reuse without inheritance (composition / HAS-A)                     | Polymorphism, loose coupling, testability                                            |
| **How it works**    | One class **uses another class/object** internally to perform a task     | Class `implements` the interface and provides method bodies                          |
| **Language level**  | Design pattern / technique (Java has **no** `delegate` keyword like C#)  | First-class Java construct (`interface`)                                             |
| **Example in Java** | `LoggerService` delegates logging to a `FileLogger`                      | A `Vehicle` interface implemented by `Car`, `Bike`                                   |


────────────────────────────────────────
🔁 Simple Example: Delegation

```java
class Printer {
    void print(String text) {
        System.out.println("Printing: " + text);
    }
}

class Document {
    private final Printer printer = new Printer(); // Delegation (HAS-A)

    void printDocument() {
        printer.print("Hello from Document!");
    }
}
```

➡️ `Document` doesn't implement printing itself. It delegates to `Printer`.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔁 Simple Example: Interface

```java
interface Printer {
    void print(String text);
}

class FilePrinter implements Printer {
    public void print(String text) {
        System.out.println("File Print: " + text);
    }
}

class ScreenPrinter implements Printer {
    public void print(String text) {
        System.out.println("Screen Print: " + text);
    }
}
```

➡️ Callers depend on `Printer` (contract), not a concrete class.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ When to Use

| Scenario                                                     | Use                                      |
| ------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------- |
| You want **code reuse without inheritance**                  | Use **Delegation**                       |
| You want to **define shared behavior** for different classes | Use **Interfaces**                       |
| You want a pattern like **Strategy**                         | Use **Interfaces + Delegation** together |
| You want to **swap behaviors at runtime**                    | Use **Delegation** with **interfaces**   |


────────────────────────────────────────
🎯 Bonus: Use Both Together (best practice)

```java
interface PaymentProcessor {
    void process(double amount);
}

class PayPalProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
    public void process(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Processing with PayPal: $" + amount);
    }
}

class PaymentService {
    private final PaymentProcessor processor;

    public PaymentService(PaymentProcessor processor) {
        this.processor = processor; // Delegation to interface type
    }

    public void pay(double amount) {
        processor.process(amount);
    }
}
```

➡️ Here:
- `PaymentProcessor` = interface (contract + polymorphism)
- `PaymentService` = delegates work to the processor (can swap PayPal/Stripe at runtime)

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Summary

| Feature            | Delegate                        | Interface                             |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| Concept            | One object uses another         | Class agrees to a contract            |
| Goal               | Reuse, avoid inheritance        | Define structure, enable polymorphism |
| Real-world Analogy | Manager gives task to assistant | Job description everyone must follow  |

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) Delegation ≠ inheritance. It is closer to **composition** (HAS-A).
2) Java has **no** `delegate` keyword (unlike C#); it’s a design technique.
3) Interface alone doesn’t “delegate” — delegation happens when an object **forwards** a call.
4) Best design often uses **both**: depend on an interface, delegate to a concrete implementation.
5) After Java 8, interfaces can have `default`/`static` methods — not always “implement every method from scratch.”
